<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Ross</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Ross is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-barrow">River Barrow</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a>, in south-east Ireland. It was developed around 1200 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>, lord of Leinster, as a port, and became one of the leading harbours of medieval Ireland. During the 1798 rebellion it was the scene of a major battle in which government troops repulsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-ross.jpg" alt="New Ross" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Ross_-_aerial_-_2026-04-18_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New Ross lies in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-barrow">River Barrow</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kilkenny">County Kilkenny</a>, 20 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>. Its Irish name is Ros Mhic Thriúin, and it was formerly known as Rosspontum or Rosspont. It is Ireland's only inland port, 32 km from the sea; the port brought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, grain, fertiliser and wood, and still takes small to medium bulk cargo vessels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement dates to the 6th century, when St Abban of Magheranoidhe founded a monastery in what is now Irishtown. The area lay in the territory of Dermot McMurrough and came to prominence when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> conquered it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> and his bride Isabella de Clare arrived early in the 13th century, and a motte was built at Old Ross to hold the territory, with a medieval borough of English and Welsh settlers around it. The town grew around the bridge William Marshal built, and received a royal charter around 1279.</p><p>The town was fought over in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a> of the 1640s. In 1643 it resisted a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-butler-1st-duke-of-ormonde">James Butler, 1st Duke of Ormond</a>, who fought the Battle of New Ross with an Irish army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-preston-1st-viscount-tara">Thomas Preston, 1st Viscount Tara</a>. Oliver Cromwell reconquered it in 1649, discharging three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shots at the Aldgate, now called the Three Bullet Gate.</p><p>The battle of New Ross on 6 June 1798 was one of the bloodiest of the rebellion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a> attacked from their camps on Carrigbyrne Hill and Lacken Hill and defeated the garrison, but British reinforcements returned later in the day, took the rebels by surprise and recaptured the town. It was the first major defeat the rebels faced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>New Ross had a population of 8,610 in 2022, making it the fourth-largest town in the county. It is home to the Dunbrody replica famine ship and the Ros Tapestry Project, a series of 15 embroidered panels made by about 150 volunteers, which depict among other subjects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>'s founding of the town. The Kennedy family homestead, the ancestral home of US President John F Kennedy, lies 8 km to the south.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Ross” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>William Marshal, who married Strongbow's daughter Isabel, developed <b>New Ross</b>, Kilkenny and many abbeys, and granted Kilkenny its charter.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leinster">Lordship of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
